{
  "summary": "Barr, an investment partner at Amplify, presents results from the 2026 State of AI Engineering survey (1,048 respondents, run in partnership with Notion and Vercel). The data-heavy talk tracks year-over-year shifts: image generation adoption doubled, audio has the highest intent-to-adopt for the second year running, cost has become a first-class engineering constraint that three in four respondents actively manage, and open-weight models augment rather than replace closed models. The headline finding is on agents: 95% of respondents now use them and 89% of those agents have write access, tripling the share of write-enabled agents year over year — while control mechanisms remain primitive human-in-the-loop approvals and permission gating. Evals remain the number one stack challenge for the third consecutive year, though by a shrinking margin, and 96% of respondents report a problem somewhere in their stack. Worth watching if you want a quantitative snapshot of what AI engineers are actually building with, buying versus building, and betting on.",
  "key_points": [
    "The survey drew 1,048 respondents and shows AI engineering is a discipline rather than a job title, spanning founders, CTOs, product people, and engineers across company sizes.",
    "For the third year running, respondents skew senior in software but new to AI: over half of those with 10+ years of software experience have three years or less of AI experience.",
    "Image generation saw the biggest year-over-year jump, with respondents building on it doubling from 18% to 36%, while audio has the strongest intent to adopt at 56% (up from 37% last year).",
    "94% use closed models and 45% use open-weight models, but over 90% of open-weight users also use closed models — open weights augment rather than replace.",
    "Open versus closed was a top-three model selection criterion for only 5% of respondents; quality dominates, followed by agentic capabilities and cost, while only one in five named reliability.",
    "Cost has become a first-class engineering constraint: 40% say it regularly shapes how ambitiously they use AI and another 36% say it sometimes does, and cost/token usage is the number two thing monitored in production after quality.",
    "Agent usage roughly doubled to 95%, and write access among agent builders jumped from 52% to 89%, tripling the overall share of write-enabled agents — yet control is still mostly human-in-the-loop approvals and permission gating.",
    "Evals are the number one stack challenge every year the survey has run, but the margin is narrowing and 96% report some stack problem; vibe review remains the most common evaluation method.",
    "Build-vs-buy splits cleanly: inference and model serving is the layer people buy most, while 61% build prompt management themselves and product-logic layers (prompts, RAG, eval) stay in-house.",
    "97% report a net positive organizational effect — chiefly cheaper failure and more experimentation — but over 9 in 10 also report negative downstream effects like erosion of deep technical skills and codebase understanding, and 81% say AI is blurring role boundaries."
  ],
  "notable_quotes": [
    {
      "text": "Every year, we see that AI engineering is more of a discipline than a job title.",
      "timestamp_sec": 115,
      "why": "Frames the survey's central definitional finding about who AI engineers are."
    },
    {
      "text": "Of those with over 10 years of software experience over half have three years or less of AI experience which tracks uh these are very experienced engineers learning a new paradigm in real time.",
      "timestamp_sec": 154,
      "why": "Reports the experience-skew number that has held for three years running."
    },
    {
      "text": "Among AI engineers who are not building with audio today, a whopping 56% say they plan to adopt it in the AI applications they build.",
      "timestamp_sec": 238,
      "why": "The headline intent-to-adopt statistic and the metric the speaker invented for the survey."
    },
    {
      "text": "The share of respondents using generative AI for images and feeling really good about it doubled from 18% last year to 36% this year.",
      "timestamp_sec": 287,
      "why": "Quantifies the largest year-over-year modality shift."
    },
    {
      "text": "What used to feel like an efficient way to generate cursed hands is just increasingly becoming a part of real work.",
      "timestamp_sec": 287,
      "why": "Memorable framing of image generation crossing the usability threshold."
    },
    {
      "text": "94% use closed models. 45% are using openweight models. But here's the thing, you know, openweight models are not replacing closed models for the most part.",
      "timestamp_sec": 329,
      "why": "Directly takes a side in the open-vs-closed debate with numbers behind it."
    },
    {
      "text": "despite the airtime of the open versus closed, it's not what drives model choice. It was a top three consideration for only 5% of the respondents.",
      "timestamp_sec": 368,
      "why": "Contrarian finding that a highly-discussed axis barely matters to practitioners."
    },
    {
      "text": "one thing that I found very interesting is that reliability is not near the top. Only one in five named reliability.",
      "timestamp_sec": 413,
      "why": "Surprising result the speaker interprets as reliability becoming a threshold requirement."
    },
    {
      "text": "it turns out that infinite intelligence still comes with a usagebased bill.",
      "timestamp_sec": 496,
      "why": "The talk's sharpest line on cost as a constraint."
    },
    {
      "text": "40% of respondents say that cost regularly shapes how ambitiously they use AI and another 36% say that it sometimes does.",
      "timestamp_sec": 496,
      "why": "Core evidence that cost is now a product-shaping engineering constraint."
    },
    {
      "text": "Last year, 52% of folks building with agents said their agents could actually write data. This year, that number is 89%.",
      "timestamp_sec": 588,
      "why": "The single biggest year-over-year shift reported in the survey."
    },
    {
      "text": "Agents are no longer reading, summarizing, drafting. They're taking actions inside of systems.",
      "timestamp_sec": 652,
      "why": "States the qualitative meaning of the write-access jump."
    },
    {
      "text": "The top two are human in the loop approvals and gating permissions which are the right instincts but kind of the same toolkit you'd use to manage an intern.",
      "timestamp_sec": 652,
      "why": "Names the gap between agent capability and agent control tooling."
    },
    {
      "text": "Nobody has settled the control layer for agents.",
      "timestamp_sec": 652,
      "why": "A clean, checkable claim about an unsolved infrastructure layer."
    },
    {
      "text": "when agents fail or when people complain about agents failing to be more precise, it's usually the thinking, not the plumbing.",
      "timestamp_sec": 695,
      "why": "Locates agent failure modes in reasoning rather than infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "text": "Every single year that I ask this, the answer, the number one answer is eval.",
      "timestamp_sec": 732,
      "why": "The talk's titular finding, with multi-year consistency behind it."
    },
    {
      "text": "96% of the people in the survey in this room have a problem with the stack. You just can't agree on which one.",
      "timestamp_sec": 732,
      "why": "Reframes the eval result as a fragmented-pain-points map for builders."
    },
    {
      "text": "prompt management is the opposite. 61% build it themselves. Um apparently everyone's prompts are special.",
      "timestamp_sec": 770,
      "why": "Concrete build-vs-buy number with the speaker's editorial read."
    },
    {
      "text": "The top effect isn't really just speed. It's cheaper failure, more experimentation, more prototypes, more bets. It didn't just make engineers faster, but it made trying things nearly free.",
      "timestamp_sec": 858,
      "why": "Reframes AI's org-level benefit away from the usual velocity narrative."
    },
    {
      "text": "today over a third of teams have non-developers shipping features, which was pretty wild to me.",
      "timestamp_sec": 914,
      "why": "Quantifies how far shipping has decoupled from the engineer role."
    },
    {
      "text": "67% expect a leading lab will declare AGI in the next five years. Note the wording. We said will, we asked about the press release, not the achievement.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1013,
      "why": "A carefully-caveated forecast number that is easy to misread without the caveat."
    },
    {
      "text": "only 9% bet on Transformers being state-of-the-art in 5 years.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1057,
      "why": "Striking long-horizon architectural bet from practitioners."
    }
  ],
